description:
`memory.rs:196-210`:

    fn recompute_task_lists_with_tasks(&mut self, account_id: &str) {
        let tasks = self
            .objects
            .get(&("Task", account_id.to_owned()))
            .cloned()
            .unwrap_or_default();

        let mut set: HashSet<Id> = HashSet::new();
        for value in tasks.values() {
            if let Some(s) = value.get("taskListId").and_then(|v| v.as_str()) {
                set.insert(Id::from(s));
            }
        }
        self.aux_mut(account_id).task_lists_with_tasks = set;
    }

The `.cloned()` on an `Option<&HashMap<Id, Value>>` materialises a fresh `HashMap<Id, Value>` — every Id, every JSON Value — solely to release the immutable borrow on `self.objects` so the subsequent `self.aux_mut(account_id)` mutable borrow compiles. The clone is gratuitous: an immutable scope can be opened over `self.objects` and closed (by ending the block) before `aux_mut` is called:

    fn recompute_task_lists_with_tasks(&mut self, account_id: &str) {
        let mut set: HashSet<Id> = HashSet::new();
        if let Some(tasks) = self.objects.get(&("Task", account_id.to_owned())) {
            for value in tasks.values() {
                if let Some(s) = value.get("taskListId").and_then(|v| v.as_str()) {
                    set.insert(Id::from(s));
                }
            }
        }
        self.aux_mut(account_id).task_lists_with_tasks = set;
    }

The immutable borrow on `self.objects` is dropped at the end of the `if let`; the `aux_mut` call that follows then has an exclusive borrow on `self`. No clone, no double-walk of the data.

What goes wrong if it stays as-is: every Task mutation (create/update/destroy/copy_task) calls `recompute_task_lists_with_tasks`, which clones every stored Task in the account just to scan their `taskListId` fields. The base reviewer flagged the O(n) algorithm separately (JMAP-h47t.10) — this finding is about the additional O(n) allocation overhead from the unnecessary clone. The two findings reinforce each other: the gratuitous clone makes the bad asymptotic worse by a constant factor.

This is the canonical 'clone to escape the borrow checker' smell. The fix is to rethink the borrow scope, not to clone harder.

Workspace-wide context: the same pattern lives in `jmap-calendars-server::memory::recompute_calendars_with_events` and `jmap-contacts-server::memory::recompute_address_books_with_contents`. Per AGENTS.md the canonical sibling for derived-index design is jmap-calendars-server (JMAP-hwdv.5 pioneered the pattern). A workspace-wide propagation epic would address all three; alternatively this crate can lead the fix and propagate.
